WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.065
Suppose you're writing a drawing program,

00:00:03.065 --> 00:00:05.020
and it shows lots of circles.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.460
Circle 1 has radius 3, and it's blue.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.717
Circle 2 has radius 5, and it's orange.

00:00:11.717 --> 00:00:14.890
And each circle can tell you its area.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.800
What do these two circles have in common?

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:22.321
They store the same kind of data: a radius and a fill color.

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:25.564
And they can do the same things: compute an area.

00:00:25.564 --> 00:00:27.550
Only the values are different.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.424
So we write the common part just one time, as a blueprint.

00:00:32.424 --> 00:00:37.728
A circle has a radius and a fill color, and it can compute its area.

00:00:37.728 --> 00:00:43.500
Then we make as many circles from it as we like, each with its own values.

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:50.765
In Python, the blueprint is called a class, and each circle made from it is an object.

00:00:50.765 --> 00:00:56.507
Welcome to Lecture 7 of Introduction to AI Programming: objects and classes.

00:00:56.857 --> 00:01:00.646
In this first part, we'll look at what an object is,

00:01:00.646 --> 00:01:06.257
what a variable really holds, and how a class describes many objects at once.

00:01:07.034 --> 00:01:13.751
An object represents something in the real world that can be told apart from everything else.

00:01:13.751 --> 00:01:17.434
A student, a desk, a circle, a button, even a loan.

00:01:17.784 --> 00:01:20.024
Every object has three things.

00:01:20.374 --> 00:01:25.222
First, an identity, which tells it apart from every other object,

00:01:25.222 --> 00:01:27.534
like a person's student number.

00:01:28.134 --> 00:01:32.623
Second, a state: the current values of its data fields.

00:01:32.623 --> 00:01:35.806
A circle's radius is part of its state.

00:01:35.806 --> 00:01:40.214
Data fields are also called properties, or attributes.

00:01:40.814 --> 00:01:47.345
And third, behavior: the actions it can perform, which are defined by its methods.

00:01:47.345 --> 00:01:49.734
A circle can compute its area.

00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:53.646
You've been using objects since Lecture 2.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:59.261
In Python, everything is an object: every number, every string, every list.

00:01:59.611 --> 00:02:04.029
The built-in `type` function tells you what type an object is.

00:02:04.029 --> 00:02:05.811
The number 3 is an `int`.

00:02:05.811 --> 00:02:08.091
The string "Welcome" is a `str`.

00:02:08.441 --> 00:02:11.632
And the `id` function gives an object's identity.

00:02:11.632 --> 00:02:15.735
It's an integer that Python assigns when the object is created,

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:18.601
and it never changes while the program runs.

00:02:18.951 --> 00:02:24.855
The actual number is different every time you run the program, so it doesn't matter what it is.

00:02:24.855 --> 00:02:29.951
What matters is that two objects alive at the same time never share the same `id`.

00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:34.814
Now, here's the most important picture in this lecture.

00:02:34.814 --> 00:02:38.894
A variable doesn't contain an object. It refers to one.

00:02:39.494 --> 00:02:41.793
Think of a name as an arrow.

00:02:41.793 --> 00:02:48.772
When we write `a` equals the list `1, 2`, Python first makes a list object in memory.

00:02:48.772 --> 00:02:51.974
Then it makes the name `a` point at it.

00:02:52.324 --> 00:02:53.905
Now, `b` equals `a`.

00:02:53.905 --> 00:02:57.698
This doesn't copy the list. It copies the arrow.

00:02:57.698 --> 00:03:02.124
There's still only one list, and both names point at it.

00:03:02.474 --> 00:03:04.174
So their `id`s are equal.

00:03:04.174 --> 00:03:10.634
And the `is` operator, which asks whether two names refer to the very same object, says `True`.

00:03:10.984 --> 00:03:14.293
Next, `c` equals a new list, `1, 2`.

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:18.704
That's a second list object, with its own arrow.

00:03:19.054 --> 00:03:21.176
Is `c` equal to `a`? Yes.

00:03:21.176 --> 00:03:26.947
The double equals sign compares values, and the values are the same.

00:03:26.947 --> 00:03:30.173
But is `c` the same object as `a`? No.

00:03:30.173 --> 00:03:35.774
It's a different list, that just happens to hold the same numbers.

00:03:36.568 --> 00:03:39.461
Pause the video and predict.

00:03:39.461 --> 00:03:42.664
We continue with `b.append(3)`.

00:03:42.664 --> 00:03:45.248
What are `a` and `c` now?

00:03:45.798 --> 00:03:53.875
The answer: the append goes through `b`'s arrow, to the one list that `a` and `b` share.

00:03:53.875 --> 00:03:56.078
So `a` is now `1, 2, 3`.

00:03:56.428 --> 00:03:58.128
And `c` is still `1, 2`.

00:03:58.128 --> 00:04:01.388
It's a different list. Nothing happened to it.

00:04:02.168 --> 00:04:05.931
The operations you perform on an object are called methods.

00:04:05.931 --> 00:04:09.568
A method is a function that belongs to the object's type.

00:04:09.918 --> 00:04:13.358
You call a method on an object, with a dot.

00:04:13.358 --> 00:04:16.718
`s.lower()` gives "welcome" in lower case.

00:04:16.718 --> 00:04:19.358
`s.upper()` gives it in capitals.

00:04:19.708 --> 00:04:23.720
`s.lower()` runs the `lower` method of the string type,

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.200
and the string it works on is `s`.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.213
That's the difference from a plain function like `len`.

00:04:30.213 --> 00:04:33.788
A method is always called on a particular object.

00:04:34.568 --> 00:04:38.488
Objects of the same kind are defined by a common class.

00:04:38.838 --> 00:04:42.967
Think of a recipe for apple pie, and the pies you bake from it.

00:04:42.967 --> 00:04:46.638
One recipe, many pies, and each pie has its own filling.

00:04:46.988 --> 00:04:51.748
So a class is also called a template, a blueprint, or a contract.

00:04:51.748 --> 00:04:57.388
It says which data fields every object has, and which methods it can perform.

00:04:57.738 --> 00:04:59.658
Here's a `Circle` class.

00:04:59.658 --> 00:05:07.018
Every circle has a radius, and three methods: `get_area`, `get_perimeter`, and `set_radius`.

00:05:07.368 --> 00:05:11.588
Now we make three circle objects from it,

00:05:11.588 --> 00:05:14.368
with radius 1, 25, and 125.

00:05:14.718 --> 00:05:19.135
Each object has its own values for the data fields.

00:05:19.135 --> 00:05:26.238
But the methods are written only once, in the class, and every object shares them.

00:05:26.838 --> 00:05:31.288
An object made from a class is called an instance of that class,

00:05:31.288 --> 00:05:34.000
and making one is called instantiation.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.478
The words object and instance mean the same thing.

00:05:38.268 --> 00:05:40.144
Here's another example.

00:05:40.144 --> 00:05:47.160
A class for people might have the data fields height, weight, IQ, and education level,

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.668
and the methods `eat`, `sleep`, and `work`.

00:05:51.018 --> 00:05:54.022
You and your friend are two instances of this class.

00:05:54.022 --> 00:05:56.738
You have the same fields, but different values.

00:05:57.535 --> 00:05:59.935
So why program this way?

00:06:00.485 --> 00:06:03.019
Writing real software is complicated.

00:06:03.019 --> 00:06:08.020
A whole field, software engineering, studies how to build large programs,

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:11.925
and programmers keep inventing new ways to organize code.

00:06:12.275 --> 00:06:17.064
Object-oriented programming lets your program match the problem.

00:06:17.064 --> 00:06:19.459
A game has players and monsters.

00:06:19.459 --> 00:06:21.704
A bank has accounts and loans.

00:06:21.704 --> 00:06:23.875
Each of them becomes a class.

00:06:24.225 --> 00:06:29.179
The data, and the code that works on it, live together.

00:06:29.179 --> 00:06:34.585
So you can understand, test, and change one class at a time.

00:06:35.368 --> 00:06:37.248
So, to sum up.

00:06:37.598 --> 00:06:42.038
An object has an identity, a state, and behavior.

00:06:42.388 --> 00:06:45.994
A variable is an arrow to an object.

00:06:45.994 --> 00:06:49.601
The `is` operator asks: same object?

00:06:49.601 --> 00:06:52.908
Double equals asks: equal values?

00:06:53.258 --> 00:06:54.994
A class is the blueprint.

00:06:54.994 --> 00:06:59.858
Each instance has its own data fields, and they all share the methods.

00:07:00.208 --> 00:07:01.488
Here's one to try.

00:07:01.488 --> 00:07:06.824
Without looking back, explain the difference between a class and an object,

00:07:06.824 --> 00:07:08.888
and give one example of each.

00:07:09.438 --> 00:07:13.060
In part two, we'll write our own class in Python,

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:18.678
and watch, step by step, what the `__init__` method and `self` do in memory.

